---
const fetchSource = `git clone https://github.com/bjesuiter/tuna-hue.git
cd tuna-hue`;

const buildExtension = `xcodebuild \\
  -project packages/plugin/TunaHue.xcodeproj \\
  -scheme TunaHue \\
  -configuration Release \\
  -destination 'generic/platform=macOS' \\
  -derivedDataPath packages/plugin/build \\
  build`;

const installExtension = `extensionsDir="$HOME/Library/Application Support/Tuna/ExtensionsDev"
mkdir -p "$extensionsDir"
ditto \\
  packages/plugin/build/Build/Products/Release/TunaHue.framework \\
  "$extensionsDir/TunaHue.framework"`;

const agentPrompt = `Build and install Tuna Hue Control on this Mac. Check for macOS 15+, Tuna 0.113+, and Xcode 16+ first. Run the following commands in order. Copy the framework only after the build succeeds.

${fetchSource}

${buildExtension}

${installExtension}

Tell me when the framework is installed and show me any errors you encounter. I’ll restart Tuna, make the trust decision if prompted, and pair my Hue Bridge with Connect First Hue Bridge.`;
---

<article class="guide" aria-labelledby="install-title">
  <h1 id="install-title">Install Tuna Hue Control</h1>

  <section class="install-card glass-panel glass-rainbow" aria-labelledby="store-title">
    <h2 id="store-title">Install from the extension store</h2>
    <p class="availability">Tuna Hue Control might come to the store soon.</p>
    <p>Extension developers and Tuna’s maintainer work together to review each extension by hand before it appears in the store.</p>
    <p>In the meantime, you can <a href="#agent-build">ask your agent to build it</a> or <a href="#build-from-source">build it manually</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="agent install-card glass-panel glass-rainbow" aria-labelledby="agent-build">
    <h2 id="agent-build">Let your agent build from source</h2>
    <p>Copy this prompt into a coding agent with Terminal access on the Mac where you use Tuna.</p>
    <pre class="agent-prompt" tabindex="0" aria-label="Prompt for your coding agent">{agentPrompt}</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="source" aria-labelledby="build-from-source">
    <h2 id="build-from-source">Build from source (manually)</h2>

    <div class="install-card requirements glass-panel glass-rainbow">
      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS 15 or later</li>
        <li>Tuna 0.113 or later</li>
        <li>Xcode 16 or later, opened once to finish setup</li>
        <li>A Philips Hue Bridge on the same network as your Mac</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Get the source</h3>
        <p>Open Terminal and run these commands to download the source and enter its folder.</p>
        <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Download the source"><code>{fetchSource}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Build the extension</h3>
        <p>Run this in the same Terminal window. Xcode downloads the pinned TunaKit dependency on the first build.</p>
        <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Build the extension with Xcode"><code>{buildExtension}</code></pre>
        <p>If Terminal reports that the active developer directory points to Command Line Tools, select your full Xcode installation under <strong>Xcode → Settings → Locations → Command Line Tools</strong> and rerun the build.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Copy the extension to Tuna</h3>
        <p>After the build succeeds, create Tuna’s developer extensions folder and copy the framework into it.</p>
        <pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Copy the framework into Tuna’s developer extensions folder"><code>{installExtension}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Restart Tuna and connect your bridge</h3>
        <p>Quit and reopen Tuna so it loads the framework. If Tuna asks you to trust the extension, confirm only if you trust the source you built. Open <strong>Settings → Sources</strong> and check that <strong>Hue Groups</strong> appears.</p>
        <p>Search for <strong>Hue</strong> and run <strong>Connect First Hue Bridge</strong>. If the command is missing, set <strong>Hue Groups → In global search</strong> to <strong>All</strong>.</p>
        <p>Allow local network access if macOS asks. Select your bridge, choose <strong>Continue</strong>, and press the physical link button when prompted. Once Tuna confirms the connection, search for a room or zone to control its lights.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</article>

